pand its roster of artists  adding country legend Wanda Jackson  recording Loretta Lynn and new singer  Margo price the eclectic catalog also  includes spoken word recordings like  auctioneer jerry king so if you drop the  needle here it's a cattle auction but if  you drop it here it's a tobacco auction  now 133 now pile but it 133 bye buddy  133 grams so why do you do because it's  it should exist at third man packaging  design is as important as the music  itself this is a limited edition  soundtrack to the great gatsby so these  are actually playable platinum coated  records oh these are wood sleeves yeah  they're wooden sleeves and then we we  riveted them ourselves  it's not just what's on the vinyl but  what's in it we pressed flower petals  into the record you plus flap now yeah a  recording of the late astronomer Carl  Sagan has a special look we did it on  this glow-in-the-dark we call it cosmic  slop vinyl some things that were  gimmicky some things that were beautiful  anything to capture attention of people  to bring back to the physical product  and get away from invisible music and  disposable music white who started his  career as a furniture upholsterer has  lavished the same attention to detail  on third man's Nashville headquarters  over an archway an upside-down turntable  is carved into the wood I care more  about how to design this room and I do a  lot how many of those records are going  to sell music feels like a  three-dimensional thing for you in the  sense that the artwork and everything  else around it you know I sort of lay in  bed thinking a lot of it is a waste of  my time because I don't know how much of  it really connects and follows through  and especially in the day and age that I  was dropped on planet Earth to work in  the day and age where people music is  the last thing on their mind after their  cellphone Netflix  Internet where I used to think when when  music was really number-one on people's  list I used to feel sorry for poets and  sculptors you know now I feel sorry for  ourselves because of what we're  competing against and so are when we see  teenager come in and buy a record that  just lights us all up this year vinyl is  expected to become a billion-dollar  industry third man now releases about a  record a week some captured from live  performances in the Blue Room at the  back of the headquarters where they can  record directly onto acetate  use to create the vinyl master we think  we're the only live venue in the world  where you can do this  this recording was of third man's latest  artist Lillie Mae a 25 year old fiddling  phenom Lillie Mae Rishi started  performing in the family band when she  was just three some musics literally in  your blood I think it is I think it is  at seven her older sister told her to  pick up the fiddle and obviously you  liked the fiddle I hated it  you did hear it really I hated it  but she came around and caught the ear  of white who recruited her for his  backing band she is you know sort of  that Michael Jackson of the Jackson five  she is to me that family every one of  them is talented but she's got that  something that's extra special about her  [Music]  white produced Lillie Mae's debut record  what's it like to see that album right  it was pretty wild I got to see it last  week I got to see a copy of it I was  like it was just crazy

